WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.000
We all want to eat
the kinds of foods

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.920
that make us feel
better and live longer.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.040
But there’s so much conflicting
information out there,

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.560
so many nutrition opinions.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.740
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.540
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.240
It’s my job to give you
the information you need

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.640
to make the healthiest
choices possible.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.520
Did you know that you can burn off significantly
more body fat exercising before meals,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.820
rather than after them?
Here’s our first story.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.860
What is the optimal exercise
timing for weight loss?

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.620
Is it better to exercise
in the morning or the evening?

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.020
Before breakfast
or after breakfast?

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.200
There was a Nobel Prize-
winning exercise physiologist

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.680
who said he always ran a mile
every morning before breakfast.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.800
Was he right? Let’s find out.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.320
More than a dozen experiments
have been published

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.700
comparing the amount
of fat burned

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.720
in a fasted versus fed state,
and every single one

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.840
found more fat was burned
on an empty stomach.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.140
On average, a single bout of low-
to-moderate intensity activity

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.080
before a meal burned off
three grams more fat

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.960
than the same amount
of exercise after a meal.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.440
That’s about three-quarters
of a pat of butter’s worth of fat,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.780
enough to improve
insulin sensitivity.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.660
The same amount of exercise,
but more fat loss,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.460
all because of timing.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.580
Now, just because you burn
more fat while you’re exercising

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.380
doesn’t necessarily
mean you end up

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.120
with less fat
at the end of the day.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.940
Maybe your body offsets
the extra fat loss

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.180
that occurs during exercise
with a little extra fat storage

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.000
when you finally do eat,
balancing it out.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.240
Researchers in Japan set out
to investigate the possibility

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.220
that your body makes
up for it later

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.000
by measuring 24-hour fat
balance after 100 minutes

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.060
of running either before
breakfast or after lunch.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.400
On the exercise-after-lunch day,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.160
they burned a total
of 608 calories of fat

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.360
over the course of that day.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.080
In contrast, on the exercise-
before-breakfast days,

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.720
in the same 24-hour period
they burned through

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.040
nearly 90 percent more,
1,142 calories of straight fat.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.380
So, the next day, they woke up

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.780
with about a quarter
cup of fat less

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.460
after the same amount
of exercise.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:12.840
That’s remarkable!

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.720
What about just
something like walking?

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.320
Sixty minutes before breakfast,
after lunch, or after dinner.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.020
Over the 24 hours
they exercised in the evening,

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.360
432 calories were burned off.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.520
On the afternoon exercise day,

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.740
they burned off
446 calories of fat.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.920
They also had a control day
with no exercise at all,

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.820
and on that day, they burned
through 456 fat calories.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.480
That’s disappointing—it’s like
they never walked at all.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.480
But the same amount
of exercise before breakfast

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.840
resulted in 717 calories
of fat loss.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.720
Over the course of a day
timing matters,

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.000
so much so that when it
comes to an hour of walking,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.540
exercise increases 24-hour
fat burning only

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.411
when it's performed
before breakfast.

00:02:57.411 --> 00:03:00.660
All such similar studies
on both men and women

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.720
show we burn through more fat
on the days we exercise before,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.460
rather than after eating.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.760
After reading the
chronobiology chapter though

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.040
or watching my
chronobiology videos,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.920
an alternative explanation
may spring to mind.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.380
Maybe it’s just a morning thing.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.300
Maybe it has nothing
to do with meals,

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.880
and your circadian rhythm
is just dictating the difference.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.060
No. Exercising in the morning
after breakfast

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.420
appears no better than exercising
in the evening after dinner,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.700
and exercising before
breakfast works better

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.580
than immediately after breakfast,
both still in the morning.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.060
It really does seem to be
a pre- versus post-meal effect.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.280
But why?

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.700
Carbohydrate is the preferred
fuel of the body.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.200
When you eat sugars or starches,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.540
they get broken down
and converted into blood sugar.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.720
After a meal, blood sugars rise
and your muscles are quick

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.180
to snatch it up for fuel
without having to rely

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.380
much on your energy stores.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.780
If you instead take a siesta,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.240
and your muscles have
no immediate need for energy,

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.340
the excess blood sugar from a meal

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.440
can be stored in our
muscles in the form

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.120
of glycogen for later use.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.380
Glycogen is just a bunch
of blood sugar molecules

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.660
strung together into
a mass of branches

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:10.920
that can be broken off and used

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.560
for quick bursts of energy
any time we need them.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.640
If you exercise after a meal,
your muscles can siphon off

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:19.800
some of the extra blood sugar
floating around for energy.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.680
Before a meal, your
muscles have to instead

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.480
resort to dipping
into your energy stores

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.280
and end up burning mostly

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.260
a combination
of glycogen and fat.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.680
That explains why you burn
more fat during fasted exercise,

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.840
but what about all the extra fat

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.640
burned throughout
the rest of the day?

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.480
Glycogen is more than a store.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.480
Glycogen isn’t just
an energy reserve,

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.960
but acts as a sensor capable
of activating metabolic pathways.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.540
Exercising before
breakfast can exhaust

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.060
as much as 18 percent
of your glycogen stores,

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.120
and that depletion can
act as a powerful rallying cry

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.100
to your fatty tissues
to start pulling

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.040
more of their own weight
by breaking down more fat.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.680
The lower glycogen
stores fall,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.680
the greater the sustained
24-hour fat loss.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.260
How long do you have
to go without food

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.700
in order to trigger this effect?

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.760
Six hours may be sufficient;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.040
so, it doesn’t have
to be before breakfast.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.220
If you timed it right, you could
exercise before a late lunch,

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.040
or if you have an early enough
lunch, maybe before dinner

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:21.060
when you get home from work.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:24.600
If exercise in a fasted
state isn’t possible,

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.880
does it matter what you eat?

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.360
Insulin release after a meal
appears to play a critical role

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.860
in suppressing fat breakdown,

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.100
explaining why lower
glycemic foods

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:36.318
can have less of an effect.

00:05:36.318 --> 00:05:39.000
Lentils were identified
as a promising option

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.800
for maintaining
athletic endurance

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.780
(which can take a hit
on an empty stomach)

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.360
while maintaining more
of the fat dissolution.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.440
Lentils are said to be
unlikely to be consumed

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.160
by the general population
due to 'low palatability".

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:57.840
They obviously haven’t tried
my mom’s lentil soup.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.820
It's worth mentioning
that although

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.880
there’s increased 24-hour
fat burning exercising

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.800
in a fasted state,
the only way to see

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.200
if this facilitates
weight loss over time

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:13.140
is to put it to the test,
which we’ll explore next.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:17.880
A systematic review and
meta-analysis on exercise timing

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.580
for fat metabolism found
that exercising

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.220
in a completely fasted
state may work best.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:26.100
The Japanese team that published
some of the seminal work

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.020
in this area went
as far as to assert:

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.260
“If exercise were
a pill to burn body fat,

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:34.920
it would be effective only
when taken before breakfast.”

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.860
Surveys show few people exercise

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:39.180
before breakfast, though.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:40.980
Before asking people
to make the switch,

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:45.360
we need to make sure that these
tantalizing 24-hour results

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.060
translate into weight loss
over the long-term.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:52.560
There’s a solid theoretical basis,
but you don’t know until...

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.420
you put it to the test.

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.700
In a study of experimental
weight gain,

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:00.480
volunteers were fed up to
4,500 calories a day for six weeks

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.960
while vigorously exercising
a total of 300 minutes a week,

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:08.040
either always after an overnight
fast or after a meal.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:11.940
A control group who didn’t exercise
at all gained about 6.5 pounds,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.940
compared to 3 pounds in the
exercise-after-a-meal group.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.060
The pre-meal exercise group
worked out the same amount,

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:21.480
but only gained about half
as much—1 3/4 pounds.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:24.420
What about weight loss, though?

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.060
Twenty young women
were randomized

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:28.620
into three hours
a week of before

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:29.820
or after a meal exercise.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:32.100
Same diets, same
amount of exercise, and...

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.520
disappointingly, about
same amount of weight loss.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.880
The pre-meal exercise group
did lose about an extra pound

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.720
of body fat (total weight loss
3.5 lbs. vs. 2.2 lbs.),

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.480
but this did not reach
statistical significance,

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:47.760
meaning such a small
difference could very well

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:48.840
have been due to chance.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.320
A study of six weeks
of low volume, high intensity

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.720
interval training
before or after meals

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.580
similarly failed
to show a difference.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:00.120
One explanation that’s
been offered for this failure

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.940
is that the increased fat loss
during premeal exercise

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.400
might be “neutralized”
by the lesser

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.320
diet-induced thermogenesis.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.380
In other words, because it costs
our body fewer calories

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:15.000
to process food if we eat after
compared to before physical activity.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.140
When we exercise after a meal
our body gets mixed signals.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.260
Exercise is all about mobilizing
energy stores for fuel,

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:25.680
whereas eating is more about
assimilation and storage,

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:28.020
and the metabolic
challenge presented

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.000
by the ensuing
“hormonal tug-of-war”

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.260
might be responsible for the 15
to 40 percent greater calorie cost.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:36.840
This has led some
to recommend exercising

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.660
after meals to facilitate
weight loss.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:42.540
If you do the math, though,
our body is so efficient

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:44.940
at digesting that the 15 percent
to 40 percent increase

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.060
might only come out
to be 3 to 12 calories.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:51.360
Such a slight difference
would be easily overwhelmed

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:53.280
by the huge disparity in fat loss,

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.040
as confirmed by the 24-hour
fat balance studies,

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:58.860
showing up to 500 calories
of fat-burning difference.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:01.440
I would suggest
a more reasonable

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.540
explanation might
be that the clear

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:06.600
body fat deficit on pre-meal
exercise days

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:10.620
is made up for by extra fat
storage on non-exercise days.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:14.040
Your body likes to hold on
to extra body fat if it can,

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:16.500
and so on days
you’re not driving it down

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.360
it may try to even things out.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.640
Both of the failed
weight loss studies

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.680
had people exercising
only three days a week,

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:26.160
and so, their bodies had most
of the week to compensate.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.800
The study I’d like
to see is a pre-meal

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.660
vs. post-meal exercise on all

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.760
or at least most days
of the week

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:36.120
to see if we can continue
to drive down fat stores.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:40.620
For those with diabetes, though,
you’d want to do the opposite.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:43.200
You can imagine how
that siphoning effect muscles

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.000
have on excess blood
sugar during exercise

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.920
might be great for those suffering

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.180
from elevated blood sugars.

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:50.520
And indeed, exercising
after a meal

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:51.720
can bring down blood sugars

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.180
as well as some blood
sugar-lowering drugs.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:57.780
Randomize type 2 diabetics
to a 20-minute leisurely stroll

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:01.140
(about 2 mph) before dinner
versus after dinner,

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.000
and the after-dinner
group blunted

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.680
their blood sugar
spike 30 percent.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:07.200
Same meal, same amount
of exercise,

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:09.660
but a significant effect
on blood sugar control

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.240
thanks to a little
tactical timing.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:14.220
Even just a 10-minute walk

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:15.780
after a meal may
make a difference.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:17.940
So, for those with
blood sugar problems,

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:20.880
it’s better to exercise
after meals than before them.

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:24.720
Blood sugar from a meal starts
appearing in the bloodstream

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:27.300
15 to 20 minutes
after the first bite

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:29.640
and is ramping
up by 30 minutes

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:31.800
to peak at around
the one-hour mark

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:35.040
before declining to premeal
levels within a few hours.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:37.140
So, for optimal
blood sugar control,

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.780
prediabetics and diabetics
should start exercising

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:41.760
30 minutes after
the start of a meal

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:43.860
and ideally go for an hour

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.440
to completely straddle
the blood sugar peak.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:49.500
If you had to choose a single
meal to exercise after,

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:52.080
it would be dinner,
due to the circadian rhythm

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:54.540
of blood sugar control
that wanes throughout the day.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:58.380
Ideally, then, breakfast would be
the largest meal of the day

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:03.268
and you’d exercise after that,
or exercise after every meal.

